        "titulo": "The \"синий family\" — the only real exception",
        "explicacao": "The \"синий family\" — the only real exception. In the middle of so many -ЫЙ and -ОЙ lives a small family that ends in -ИЙ and behaves differently. It is the синий family — the only real exception you need to know at the start. The most common members: Notice the difference: The feminine is -ЯЯ (not -АЯ). Си́няя, not \"си́ная\". The neuter is -ЕЕ (not -ОЕ). Си́нее, not \"си́ное\". The plural is -ИЕ (not -ЫЕ). Си́ние, not \"си́ные\". How do you recognise it? If the masculine ends in -ИЙ and the root ends in -н (or a soft consonant), it is probably from the синий family. If the root ends in г, к, х, it is just the spelling forcing -ИЙ (and the feminine is a normal -АЯ). A curiosity: almost all members of the синий family have a time meaning (parts of the day, seasons, \"last\"). That is a useful pattern for recognising them. And третий? It is the other exception. Тре́тий (third) has the feminine тре́тья (not \"тре́тия\") and the neuter тре́тье (not \"тре́тие\"). It is a linguistic fossil, a remnant of an old declension. You just have to memorise it.",
