      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 4,
        "titulo": "The big confusion: spelling -ИЙ vs. the -ИЙ of the синий family",
        "explicacao": "The big confusion: spelling -ИЙ vs. the -ИЙ of the синий family. This is a point that is often confusing. Let us take it calmly. There is a Russian spelling rule: after the letters г, к, х, ж, ч, ш, щ, Ы is never written. И is written. That is why adjectives such as ру́сский (root РУССК-), ма́ленький (root МАЛЕНЬК-), ди́кий (root ДИК-), хоро́ший (root ХОРОШ-), горя́чий (root ГОРЯЧ-) end in -ИЙ in the masculine. But they are not from the синий family. And how do they behave in the feminine and the neuter? Like ordinary adjectives: ру́сский → ру́сская → ру́сское ✅ ма́ленький → ма́ленькая → ма́ленькое ✅ ди́кий → ди́кая → ди́кое ✅ хоро́ший → хоро́шая → хоро́шее ✅ (by the 5-sibilant rule) горя́чий → горя́чая → горя́чее ✅ (by the 5-sibilant rule) That is: it is NOT -яя/-ее. It is -ая/-ое like any ordinary adjective. The ending -ИЙ is only spelling. The rule that separates the two groups: if the root ends in г, к, х, ж, ч, ш, щ → the -ИЙ is a spelling one → feminine in -АЯ, neuter in -ОЕ, plural in -ИЕ. If the root ends in -н or another soft consonant (the синий family) → the -ИЙ is \"real\" → feminine in -ЯЯ, neuter in -ЕЕ, plural in -ИЕ. How do you tell which is which in practice? Look at the feminine. If you see \"-ая\", it is ordinary. If you see \"-яя\", it is from the синий family. With time you recognise it. A memory trick: the синий family is small. There are about 15-20 adjectives, almost all with a time meaning (seasons, parts of the day) or \"last\". Everything else that ends in -ИЙ is a normal -АЯ/-ОЕ.",
