        "titulo": "Possessives, «этот» and the owner",
        "explicacao": "The possessive agrees with the thing owned, not with the owner: «моя книга» is said by a man and by a woman alike. In English «my» never changes, so this is new: Russian possessives change with the gender of the thing. The forms «его» (his), «её» (her) and «их» (their) never change. To say who owns something, put the owner's name in the genitive (the form used after «нет»): «сумка Лены» = Lena's bag (English uses 's for the same job).",
